import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var a={coulombLaw:{theory:{concept:"[[Coulomb's law: two point charges at rest act on each other with a force directly proportional to the product of their charges and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them: F = k \xB7 q\u2081 \xB7 q\u2082 / r\xB2.]] The constant k \u2248 8.99 \xB7 10\u2079 N\xB7m\xB2/C\xB2 holds in a vacuum; in a medium with permittivity \u03B5 the force is \u03B5 times weaker."},section:{history:{title:"How the law was found",text:"In 1785 the French physicist Charles-Augustin de Coulomb measured the force between small charged balls with a torsion balance: a thin horizontal rod hanging on a very fine thread. The force turned the rod and twisted the thread, and the angle of the twist measured the force. He changed the distance and the charges, and found the rule that now carries his name. The unit of charge, the coulomb (C), is named after him."},law:{title:"Coulomb's law",text:"The force between two small charged bodies is F = k\xB7q\u2081\xB7q\u2082 / r\xB2, where q\u2081 and q\u2082 are the charges, r is the distance between their centres and k = 8.99\xB710\u2079 N\xB7m\xB2/C\xB2 is the Coulomb constant. Double one charge and the force doubles; double both and it grows four times. In the simulation, F\xB7r\xB2 stays the same all along the sweep \u2014 that constant is k\xB7q\u2081\xB7q\u2082."},direction:{title:"Repulsion and attraction",text:"[[Like charges (+ + or \u2212 \u2212) repel each other, unlike charges (+ \u2212) attract.]] The force acts along the line joining the charges, and by Newton's third law the two bodies feel forces of the same size and opposite directions \u2014 however different their charges are. A neutral body (q = 0) feels no electric force from a point charge."},inverse:{title:"The inverse-square law",text:"[[Double the distance and the force becomes four times smaller; triple it and the force is nine times smaller; halve the distance and the force is four times bigger.]] Watch the test charge slide along the ruler: the force falls steeply at first and then flattens out. The graph of F against r is not a straight line but this curve, and F\xB7r\xB2 on the third graph is constant."},size:{title:"A coulomb is enormous",text:"Two charges of 1 C at a distance of 1 m would push each other with about 9 billion newtons \u2014 about the weight of a million tonnes. Real lab charges are millionths of a coulomb (microcoulombs, \xB5C): two 1 \xB5C balls 10 cm apart push with 0.9 N. That is why ordinary objects, with equal amounts of positive and negative charge, are electrically neutral."},medium:{title:"Charges in a medium",text:"If the charges are not in a vacuum but in a material, the force is divided by the relative permittivity \u03B5 of the material: F = k\xB7q\u2081\xB7q\u2082 / (\u03B5\xB7r\xB2). For air \u03B5 \u2248 1, for oil about 2, for glass about 6 and for water about 80. In water the force between ions is 80 times weaker \u2014 which is why salt falls apart into separate ions when it dissolves."},superposition:{title:"Several charges",text:"When there are more charges, each pair acts as if the others were not there, and the forces on a charge add as vectors: F = F\u2081 + F\u2083. With two equal charges on the ends, a test charge in the middle is pushed equally from both sides, and the net force is zero. If the ends carry different charges, the balance point is closer to the weaker one: r/(d \u2212 r) = \u221A(q\u2081/q\u2083). Switch on the third charge and look for the point where the net force line crosses zero on the graph."},gravity:{title:"Electricity against gravity",text:"Newton's law of gravitation has the same 1/r\xB2 form, but electric forces are incomparably stronger: for two small charged balls the electric force can be 10\xB9\u2076 times the force of gravity. We do not feel it between ourselves because ordinary bodies are neutral; gravity wins only on the scale of planets, where matter is neutral on average and mass adds up."},check:{title:"Check your understanding",text:"The distance is doubled. What happens to the force? (It becomes 4 times smaller.) Both charges are doubled. (The force becomes 4 times larger.) The same charges are put into water. (The force becomes 80 times smaller.) Two like charges: where is the net force on a third charge zero? (Between them, closer to the weaker one.) Test each answer in the simulation."}},formula:{law:"Coulomb's law: the force between two point charges",constant:"The Coulomb constant (equal to 1/(4\u03C0\xB7\u03B5\u2080))",ratio:"Inverse square: the forces at two distances",medium:"In a medium of relative permittivity \u03B5 the force is weaker",superposition:"With several charges the forces add as vectors",balance:"Where the net force on a test charge between q\u2081 and q\u2083 is zero (d: distance between q\u2081 and q\u2083)",gravity:"The electric force compared with gravity between the same two bodies (G: gravitational constant)",energy:"Energy of two charges, and the force as its slope"},variable:{q:"Electric charge",r:"Distance between the charges",F:"Force",k:"Coulomb constant",epsilon:"Relative permittivity of the medium"},assumption:{points:"The charged bodies act as points and do not move; no charge flows between them or is induced on them.",steady:"The test charge is moved slowly by hand (a prescribed slide), not by the force: this is a static measurement, nothing accelerates.",medium:"The medium simply divides the force by \u03B5; real water also screens charges and its molecules move around.",scale:"Not to scale: the balls are drawn far larger than point charges, and the arrows show the force in proportion to the largest force possible in the lab in the same medium (the numbers give the exact value)."},param:{mode:"Test charge q\u2082",charge1:"Charge q\u2081 (fixed)",charge2:"Charge q\u2082 (test charge)",charge3:"Charge q\u2083 (fixed)",distance:"Distance r from q\u2081",third:"Add a third charge",distance3:"Distance of q\u2083 from q\u2081",medium:"Medium",sweepPeriod:"Time of one slide there and back",mass1:"Mass of body 1",mass2:"Mass of body 2",timeStep:"Time step"},hint:{mode:"Slides along the ruler by itself, or stays where you put it.",charge2:"The sign matters: the same sign repels, the opposite sign attracts.",distance:"Measured from q\u2081 to q\u2082, in centimetres.",third:"Then the net force on q\u2082 is the sum of two forces.",medium:"The medium weakens the force: water by a factor of about 80.",sweepPeriod:"How long the test charge needs to slide out and back.",mass:"Used only to compare the electric force with gravity."},preset:{sweep:"Slides by itself",fixed:"Stays where I put it",neg5:"\u22125 \xB5C",neutral:"Neutral",pos5:"+5 \xB5C",cm15:"15 cm",cm20:"20 cm",cm40:"40 cm",cm80:"80 cm"},medium:{vacuum:"Vacuum",air:"Air",oil:"Oil",glass:"Glass",water:"Water"},measure:{force:"Force on q\u2082 (size)",distance:"Distance from q\u2081 to q\u2082",force1:"Force on q\u2082 from q\u2081 (+ pushes away)",force3:"Force on q\u2082 from q\u2083 (+ pushes right)",netForce:"Net force on q\u2082 (+ points right)",epsilon:"Relative permittivity \u03B5",energy:"Energy of q\u2082 in the field",invSquare:"Force \xD7 distance\xB2, N\xB7m\xB2",ratioLog:"Electric force / gravity (power of ten)",kEffective:"k / \u03B5 in 10\u2079 N\xB7m\xB2/C\xB2"},view:{status:"{kind} \xB7 {medium} (\u03B5 = {epsilon})",repulsion:"Repulsion: like charges push each other apart",attraction:"Attraction: unlike charges pull together",none:"No force: a neutral body feels nothing",netRight:"Net force on q\u2082 points to the right",netLeft:"Net force on q\u2082 points to the left",netZero:"Net force on q\u2082 is zero: balance",formula:"F = k\xB7q\u2081\xB7q\u2082 / r\xB2 = 8.99\xB710\u2079 \xD7 {q1} \xD7 {q2} / ({r})\xB2 = {f}",formulaMedium:"F = k\xB7q\u2081\xB7q\u2082 / (\u03B5\xB7r\xB2) = 8.99\xB710\u2079 \xD7 {q1} \xD7 {q2} / ({epsilon} \xD7 ({r})\xB2) = {f}",formulaThird:"F = F\u2081 + F\u2083 = ({f1}) + ({f3}) = {net}"},graph:{forceDistance:"Force against distance",superposition:"Forces on q\u2082: from q\u2081, from q\u2083 and the sum",invSquare:"Force \xD7 distance\xB2 against distance (constant)"},challenge:{tenNewtons:{title:"Ten newtons",description:"Hold the test charge in place (not sliding) and make the force on it 10 N, give or take 0.5 N.",hint:'Switch q\u2082 to "Stays where I put it", bring the charges closer and make them bigger: F = k\xB7q\u2081\xB7q\u2082/r\xB2.'},attraction:{title:"A strong attraction",description:"Hold the test charge in place and make q\u2082 attract q\u2081 with a force of at least 5 N.",hint:"The charges need opposite signs. Use big charges and a short distance."},farStrong:{title:"Strong from far away",description:"Hold the test charge at least 60 cm from q\u2081 and still make the force 1 N or more.",hint:"Far away the force is weak, so both charges must be big: about 6.5 \xB5C each."},water:{title:"A strong force in water",description:"Put the charges in water and still make the force at least 0.5 N (test charge held in place).",hint:"Water divides the force by 80: you need much bigger charges or a short distance."},balance:{title:"Balance between two charges",description:"Add a third charge and place q\u2082 so that the net force on it is zero, while the single forces are not.",hint:"Give q\u2081 and q\u2083 the same sign and charge, and put q\u2082 halfway between them."},unequal:{title:"The balance point moves",description:"Make q\u2081 at least 3 times larger than q\u2083 (same sign), and find the point where the net force on q\u2082 is zero.",hint:"The balance point is closer to the weaker charge: r/(d \u2212 r) = \u221A(q\u2081/q\u2083). For q\u2081 = 4\xB7q\u2083 it is 2/3 of the way."},gravity:{title:"Stronger than gravity",description:"Choose charges and masses so that the electric force is 10\xB9\u2076 times stronger than gravity between the same bodies.",hint:"Use the biggest charges and the smallest masses (1 g)."}}}},r={sim:a};export{r as default,a as sim};
